comments.body:
You're right that there's a second bug, and it's in Fyne, not downstream. It predates #6465 — the cache-deadline regression just makes it easy to hit.

**A renderer recreated after cache expiry is never laid out.**

`cache.Renderer` creates a renderer when one is missing but never calls `Layout` on it, and `BaseWidget.Resize` returns early when the size is unchanged — which it always is in this situation, since only the renderer was thrown away. So nothing lays the new renderer out.

That matters because most widgets rebuild their canvas objects in `CreateRenderer`. `widget.Label` is the clearest case:

```go
func (l *Label) CreateRenderer() fyne.WidgetRenderer {
	l.provider = NewRichTextWithText(l.Text) // fresh, size {0,0}
	...
}

func (r *labelRenderer) Layout(s fyne.Size) {
	r.l.selection.Resize(s)
	r.l.provider.Resize(s) // the only thing that ever sizes the provider
}
```

`labelRenderer.Refresh` doesn't size the provider either, so the label keeps a zero-sized `RichText` until something resizes the label to a *different* size. With `TextAlignCenter` the text is then centred inside width 0 and lands half its width to the left of where it belongs. Same label, before and after one `Capture()` in the downstream failure:

```
before: *widget.Label    size={139.69 35.08}
          *widget.RichText size={139.69 35.08}
            *canvas.Text   pos={8 8}      abs={102.16 156.46}

after:  *widget.Label    size={139.69 35.08}
          *widget.RichText size={0 0}
            *canvas.Text   pos={-61.84 8} abs={32.31 156.46}
```

That is the entire difference in the golden-image failure; no downstream state is involved.

**Minimal reproduction, no downstream code.** Fails on 2.8.1, 2.8.0, 2.7.1 and 2.6.3, so it is independent of the cache-deadline bug. Run with `FYNE_CACHE=10ms`:

```go
func TestLabelLosesLayoutWhenRendererIsRecreated(t *testing.T) {
	_ = test.NewApp()

	label := widget.NewLabelWithStyle("Drop images here", fyne.TextAlignCenter, fyne.TextStyle{Bold: true})
	w := test.NewWindow(label)
	w.Resize(fyne.NewSize(300, 100))

	before := test.WidgetRenderer(label).Objects()[0].Size() // the RichText provider

	// Idle past cache.ValidDuration, then paint: Capture calls cache.Clean(true),
	// which destroys the expired renderer, and the paint that follows recreates it.
	time.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)
	w.Canvas().Capture()

	after := test.WidgetRenderer(label).Objects()[0].Size()
	assert.Equal(t, before, after) // {292 92} -> {0 0}
}
```

**How the two compose.** On its own this is largely invisible, because a visible widget is marked alive by every paint and so never expires. The deadline bug removes that protection: `drawSingleFrame` paints first and calls `cache.Clean` last, so after an idle gap a frame marks visible widgets alive from the stale `cachedNow`, `Clean` destroys them, and the next frame recreates their renderers unlaid-out. The mis-render then persists until something resizes those widgets.

**Candidate fix** — lay out on creation when the widget already has a size:

```go
func Renderer(wid fyne.Widget) fyne.WidgetRenderer {
	renderer, ok := CachedRenderer(wid)
	if !ok && wid != nil {
		renderer = wid.CreateRenderer()
		rinfo := &rendererInfo{renderer: renderer}
		rinfo.setAlive()
		renderers.Store(wid, rinfo)

		// A widget that already has a size is being re-rendered after its
		// previous renderer was destroyed; Resize will not lay it out again.
		if size := wid.Size(); !size.IsZero() {
			renderer.Layout(size)
		}
	}

	return renderer
}
```

With only that patch on top of 2.8.1 — the cache-deadline bug still present — the test above passes and the downstream suite passes under `FYNE_CACHE=100ms`. Happy to open a PR for it separately from the deadline fix if that's useful.
